					Chapter 1

INTRODUCTION

Project Context	

Disasters are a growing global concern, occurring with increasing frequency and intensity due to climate change, globalization, and rapid urbanization. Around the world, emergency management and health agencies play critical roles during disaster events, yet studies reveal that communication breakdowns between these sectors often hinder timely and effective response. Barriers such as unclear protocols, delayed reporting, and poor data integration limit decision-making efficiency, sometimes turning manageable incidents into large-scale crises. Global experts highlight the potential of digital tools and artificial intelligence to improve data sharing, streamline incident reporting, and support faster, better-coordinated responses (Abbas et al., 2025).

The Philippines ranks among the most disaster-prone countries in the world, with an average of 15 to 20 typhoons annually. Despite having national policies and programs aimed at disaster risk reduction management (DRRM), implementation often suffers from inefficiency, corruption, and poor inter-agency coordination. These gaps in disaster preparedness underscore the urgent need for technology-driven solutions that empower communities to respond rapidly and effectively at the local level. Strengthening barangay-level disaster reporting systems is particularly important in the Philippine context, where small-scale, community-focused action can significantly reduce risks and losses (Lallana, 2021).

In Davao City, catastrophic flooding events such as the 2011 flash flood resulted in significant property damage and loss of lives. Post-disaster studies revealed that the absence of a clear communication protocol and low community awareness contributed to a six-hour delay in the initial response, which worsened the disaster’s impact. While agencies displayed a degree of preparedness, the lack of a real-time, coordinated reporting mechanism left communities highly vulnerable. These findings emphasize the critical importance of localized disaster management solutions that prioritize immediate communication and coordination between residents and authorities (Cayamanda & Paunlagui, 2020).

Traditional disaster reporting methods in many Philippine barangays rely on manual communication, such as phone calls or in-person reports. While these methods have served communities for years, they are often too slow to support rapid response in time-sensitive situations. Delays in communication can lead to greater property loss, injuries, and fatalities. In the context of modern technology, relying solely on outdated reporting systems is no longer sufficient to protect communities from disaster impacts.

Despite global advancements in digital disaster management tools, there is a research gap in developing localized, real-time, two-way communication systems specifically tailored to barangay-level needs in the Philippines. Existing systems often fail to integrate community participation, rapid verification, and instant information dissemination to residents. This study seeks to fill that gap by developing and evaluating a web-based system designed to facilitate real-time disaster reporting, enhance communication, and improve coordination between communities and local authorities.	This study addresses a critical gap in Philippine disaster management by developing a localized, web-based system for real-time barangay-level reporting and response. Its primary contribution is an innovative two-way communication platform that empowers community participation, a key factor often missing in existing top-down models (Damaševičius et al., 2023). The research is directly relevant to national efforts to enhance local resilience, with the potential to improve response times and reduce disaster impacts through practical, technology-driven action. To ensure practical application, the study's findings and the B-READY system will be presented to barangay officials and school planning team, with a plan for controlled deployment and integration into local disaster protocols.

Purpose and Description

The purpose of this developmental study is to facilitate disaster reporting and response at the barangay level. It emphasizes real-time communication. The website would enable quick reporting of incidents and provide timely responses to disasters or emergencies within a barangay community. It improves the disaster preparedness and management localized.

Features

The Login/Signup System allows new users to create an account by providing their name or username, email address, password with confirmation, and agreeing to the terms and conditions, while returning users can log in using their username or email and password, with options for a “Remember Me” checkbox and a “Forgot Password?” link. Once logged in, users are directed to their respective dashboard.

 	The Victim Dashboard includes a header with the user’s name, SOS button, and notifications, a sidebar with options for reporting emergencies, viewing rescue requests, safety tips, and account settings, and a main content area displaying a live map of nearby rescue units, current disaster warnings, and the status of active rescue requests. 

The Rescue Team Dashboard provides a header with the team name, mission alerts, and logout, a sidebar for assigned missions, disaster zone maps, resource checklists, and team chat, and a main content area featuring a real-time map with victim pins color-coded by urgency, mission details, status update buttons, and emergency contact integration, and it also includes system settings that provide a live disaster feed, mission tracking, performance analytics, and a communication panel for broadcasting announcements.

A real-time map integrates across dashboards to show victims, rescue teams, and disaster-affected areas, updated instantly through live location tracking using the Google Maps API, GPS from rescue team devices, and victim locations via mobile GPS or manual pin-drop. 

Incident reporting is streamlined through a pre-registered user profile storing full name, home address, and contact number, which auto-fills during reports. Instead of slow dropdown menus, large clickable buttons allow quick reporting for disasters like flood, fire, earthquake, typhoon, medical emergencies, or others, with options to upload auto-compressed photos or videos for clarification.



Objectives of the Study	

This study aims to develop and evaluate "B-READY," a web-based system designed to enhance real-time disaster reporting, improve response coordination, and increase community participation at the barangay level. To achieve this, the study pursues the following specific objectives:

1. To design and develop B-READY, a web-based system that will facilitate real-time disaster reporting, enhance communication between residents and authorities, improve response efficiency, empower community participation, and strengthen localized disaster management.

2. Evaluate the effectiveness of B-READY in terms of:

a. Functional Suitability 

b. Reliability 

c. Portability 

d. Usability 

e. Performance Efficiency 

f. Security 

g. Compatibility 

h. Maintainability

Review of Related Literature

	The review of related literature provides a comprehensive overview of existing studies, theories, and insights relevant to the topic at hand. This section aims to establish the foundation for the research by examining prior works that inform, support, and contextualize the current investigation. 





Defining and Describing B-READY

Functional Suitability, Functional Suitability refers to the degree to which a system provides functions that meet stated and implied needs when used under specified conditions (Yaneza et al., 2025). For B-READY, this directly translates to features that address specific gaps in barangay-level disaster management, such as the one-click incident reporting buttons, role-based dashboards for victims and rescue teams, and the live map. These functions must accurately fulfill the real-world tasks of reporting, coordinating, and responding to emergencies as outlined in the system's objectives.

The completeness of these functions is paramount. A system's functional suitability is judged by how well its features cover the necessary tasks without omission (Gatchalian et al., 2023). In the context of B-READY, the system must comprehensively support the entire incident lifecycle from the victim's initial SOS to the rescue team's mission closure, ensuring no critical step in the process is unsupported by the software.

Furthermore, the functions must be logically sound and correct. According to Pascual et al. (2025), the accuracy of a system's operations is a key measure of its suitability. For B-READY, this means that when a victim reports a flood, the system must correctly assign the urgency level, pin the location accurately on the map, and notify the appropriate rescue units without error. Functional correctness is non-negotiable in a disaster scenario where misinformation can have serious consequences.

Reliability. Reliability is the ability of a system to perform its required functions under stated conditions for a specified period of time (Nuevas et al., 2024). For a mission-critical system like B-READY, this means maintaining 24/7 operational readiness and ensuring that the reporting, mapping, and communication functions are available and perform correctly, especially during peak usage caused by a disaster. System failure during an emergency would undermine its entire purpose and erode community trust.

A reliable system must exhibit fault tolerance, allowing it to continue operating as intended despite the presence of hardware or software faults (Del Pilar et al., 2024). B-READY must be designed to handle potential issues, such as a temporary loss of a user's GPS signal or a spike in server load, without a complete system crash, ensuring that partial functionality is maintained even under suboptimal conditions.

The capability to recover quickly from an interruption is also crucial (Gatchalian et al., 2023). In the event of a server outage or power failure, B-READY must have mechanisms to rapidly restore service and any critical data, such as pending rescue requests, to prevent the loss of life-saving information and re-establish operational status as soon as possible.

Portability, Portability is the degree of effectiveness and efficiency with which a system can be transferred from one hardware, software, or operational environment to another (Kedziora, 2025). For B-READY, this is critical as barangay responders and residents will access the system from a diverse range of devices and browsers. A highly portable web-based system ensures that all users, regardless of their personal technology, can reliably report incidents and view the real-time map, which is fundamental to the system's mission of universal accessibility during a crisis.

A key aspect of portability, adaptability, ensures that the system can be effectively installed and run in different environments without significant rework (Al-Hawamleh, 2022). This is essential for B-READY's deployment across multiple barangays, which may have varying IT infrastructure and levels of technological sophistication, ensuring it functions as a uniform tool for disaster response and eliminates technical barriers to adoption.

Furthermore, a portable architecture supports the long-term maintainability and scalability of the system. As noted by Naqvi et al. (2025), such a design is easier to update and modify. For B-READY, this means the core application can be more readily adapted to integrate new mapping services or communication protocols in the future, ensuring its continued effectiveness without requiring a complete system redesign.

Usability. Usability is the capability of the software product to be understood, learned, used, and attractive to the user, when used under specified conditions (Debnath et al., 2020). For B-READY, this means that both distressed victims and busy rescue personnel can navigate the interface intuitively under high-stress conditions, with clearly labeled buttons like the SOS and straightforward menus to access critical functions without confusion or training.

A vital aspect of usability is designing interfaces that provide user error protection (Arony et al., 2023). In B-READY, this could involve confirmation dialogs before sending an SOS or designing the incident form to auto-fill from user profiles, thereby minimizing manual data entry mistakes during a panic and preventing accidental or incorrect reports.

Furthermore, high learnability ensures that users can easily become proficient with the application with minimal time and effort (Peters & Aggrey, 2020). Given that B-READY may be used infrequently until an emergency arises, its design must be instantly recognizable and easy to recall, allowing residents and responders to use it effectively without needing to re-learn complex procedures during a disaster.

Performance Efficiency. Performance Efficiency is the ability of a software system to provide appropriate performance relative to the amount of resources used under stated conditions (Zhang, 2024). For B-READY, this quality is critical during a disaster when a surge of users simultaneously reporting incidents and tracking responses could strain the system. Efficient resource utilization ensures that the real-time map and notification services remain responsive, preventing dangerous delays in communication.

The capacity to handle the anticipated peak load of concurrent users from a barangay community is a fundamental requirement (Naqvi et al., 2025). B-READY must be designed and tested to ensure that its database, server, and network bandwidth can process a high volume of incident reports, location pings, and status updates without significant degradation in response time.

Furthermore, excellent time-behavior is a direct measure of its performance (Pakkanen, 2025). In the context of B-READY, this translates to near-instantaneous updates on the rescue team dashboard when an SOS is triggered and minimal latency in the live location tracking of both victims and responders. Slow performance could lead to misallocated resources and delayed aid, directly impacting emergency outcomes.

Security. Security is the degree to which a product protects information and data so that persons or other products or systems have the data access appropriate to their types and levels of authorization (Naqvi et al., 2025). For B-READY, this is critical because the system handles sensitive personal data, including user identities, home addresses, and real-time locations, which must be safeguarded against unauthorized access that could lead to misuse or panic.

Ensuring confidentiality is paramount, as it guarantees that data is accessible only to those authorized to access it (Alvarez et al., 2021). This requires B-READY to implement robust authentication for the login system and role-based access control to ensure that, for example, a regular victim cannot see the rescue team's internal communications, thereby maintaining data segregation and privacy.

Protecting data integrity is equally important, as it prevents information from being unauthorized modification or deletion (Zhang, 2024). In the context of B-READY, it is vital that incident reports, mission statuses, and location coordinates cannot be maliciously altered. Ensuring integrity guarantees that rescue teams are acting on accurate and trustworthy information, which is foundational for effective and safe emergency response.

Compatibility. Compatibility is the degree to which a product can exchange information with other systems, and/or perform its required functions while sharing the same hardware or software environment (Zhang, 2024). For B-READY, this is foundational, as the system's core functionality depends on seamless integration with external APIs like Google Maps for geolocation and WebSockets for real-time notifications. Any compatibility issue with these services would directly cripple the live-mapping and instant alert features, rendering the system ineffective for emergency response.

The ability to co-exist with existing software is crucial because B-READY must operate reliably alongside municipal systems and network infrastructure without causing conflicts (Chicas & Maag, 2021). In the varied technological landscape of barangays, the system must function correctly across different browsers and operating systems, preventing user exclusion due to minor environmental differences.

Finally, achieving interoperability enables B-READY to act as a central hub by effectively consuming and utilizing data from external platforms. As highlighted in a study on local governance systems, this structured integration is what transforms a standalone application into a powerful, connected tool that enhances operational efficiency (Amar, 2025). Therefore, B-READY's compatibility ensures it can provide a comprehensive solution by seamlessly working with other critical technologies. 

Maintainability. Maintainability is the effectiveness and efficiency with which a product can be modified to improve it, correct it, or adapt it to changes in environment, or requirements (Özdemir & Buzluca, 2024). For a long-term project like B-READY, this means the codebase must be structured to allow for easy updates, such as adding new disaster types or integrating new communication APIs, without requiring a complete system rewrite.

Achieving this requires a modular design, where the system is composed of segmented and loosely coupled components (Kedziora, 2025). For instance, B-READY's architecture should allow developers to update the real-time mapping module independently from the user authentication module, making the development process more agile and reducing the risk of introducing new bugs when making changes.

High testability is a crucial enabler of maintainability, as it allows for the establishment of effective and efficient test criteria (Ariningsih, 2024). A well-designed B-READY system will have automated tests for its critical functions. This allows developers to confidently make improvements or fixes, knowing that any regression in performance or functionality will be quickly identified, ensuring the system's long-term reliability.

Benefits of B-READY

	The advancement in technology has resulted in the integration of internet-compliant devices and systems in emergency response and management, known as the Internet of Emergency Services (IoES). The integration has the potential to revolutionize the provision of emergency services through facilitating real-time data analysis and collection and coordination among various agencies involved in emergency response. Emergencies may be of various types, including natural emergencies like hurricanes, earthquakes, and wildfires, and man-made emergencies like terrorist attacks, industrial accidents, and pandemics. Successful emergency management and response measures have the potential to decrease the impact of such emergencies, prevent loss of life, and minimize loss to property. Possibly the greatest reason why emergency management and response are so crucial is that emergencies can be spontaneous and may require immediate response. In most cases, emergency responders have only minutes or hours in which to act before the situation turns dangerous or deadly. By using effective emergency management plans and response protocols, emergency responders can act quickly and decisively and possibly prevent a crisis from arising (Damaševičius et al,. 2023).

	Catastrophes often propagate swiftly. Clear instructions and effective communication are crucial, as they represent the primary aspect of the necessary response and can significantly enhance public safety. This piece suggests an extensive cloud notification application that can manage a range of emergency scenarios. It delivers instant notifications and guidance to all impacted and necessary individuals via their mobile devices and computer displays within a specific organization, regardless of their location (inside or outside the organization). Additionally, these individuals do not necessarily need to possess a mobile phone with a particular operating system, like an Android smartphone as seen in much of the earlier research. This is due to the fact that the suggested system only necessitates a browser that operates on any device with any operating system, including mobile phones (Abd-Alsabour & Salib, 2022).

Existing Related Innovations to B-READY	

While few studies exist in the use of Internet of Things (IoT) in indoor disaster management scenarios, there is a significant absence of research and scalable products in voice command assistant based IoT solutions for public safety and emergency responder situations like mass transit accident, floods, active shooter, and other incidents where just-in-time response and immediate aid are essential. Public safety is the cornerstone of our research study. Social networks drive consumer adoption of IoT technology because users seek feedback from peers, family, and social media influencers to lessen IoT product or service uncertainty. Prior research has demonstrated that customer trust affects IoT adoption. With the advance in Industrial IoT (IIOT) in disaster management, we have solutioned Zenext-IoT (ZIOT) which runs on a core voice command technology for public safety personnel. The goal of this research is to evaluate the degree to which first responders are willing to use the Internet of Things-based disaster management framework that has been proposed. This research aims to solve an urgent and imminent solution of providing the target segment of public safety officials and rescue worker with hands-free voice command virtual assistant IoT model. Our target users are emergency responders and rescue workers for whom technology needs to be simple to use and just-in-time (Swarnamouli Majumdar et al, 2024).

The research introduces the design of an AI-based disaster response system that subscribes to enhance real-time emergency management. Manual processing, slow communication and siloed data sources are common downsides of traditional-approach systems, which ultimately translate into slower response times or insufficient resource spend. The system deals with them by exploiting ML, predictive analytics, and automatic decision-making so that these can consolidate pre-existing real-time information from social media, satellite images or IoT sensors. Several experiments show better-than-previous: response times decrease from an average of 11 hours to 3.6 and prediction accuracy increased from 62.5% up to cover 85% Average efficiency also improved including +23% in resource allocation. The innovative solution shifts disaster response from being entirely reactive to proactive, creating faster and more informed responses during times of crisis, thereby assuring public safety for all in the face of calamities (T. K. Lakshmi et al, 2025).

	Regardless of the extensive research conducted on large-scale evacuations, the instrumentation of these processes still represents an open issue for first response organizations. Self-evacuation of civilians that follows evacuation plans has shown to be feasible as early response to several natural disasters; however, the typical lack of interaction capability of the evacuees with first response organizations and emergency managers jeopardizes the effectiveness of these processes. This article proposes an IoT-based infrastructure that supports self-evacuation of civilians in mass processes, allowing people to participate as information providers and consumers. This infrastructure is the backbone of an ambient intelligence system used as a bridge between evacuees, first response units, and the emergency operation center managing the process. Depending on the people location and the status of the area, the system implements breadcrumbs that guide people to shelters and safe places. The proposed infrastructure includes (1) an architecture that captures the core design aspects of the solution and makes it reusable for other researchers, (2) an implementation of the system based on Raspberry Pi 3 devices with LoRa radio connectivity, (3) a mobile application that allows evacuees to interact with the evacuation system, and (4) a simplified algorithm to support the deployment of the IoT-based infrastructure into an urban area. The solution was evaluated using real measurements and simulations, and the obtained results are highly encouraging (Finochiettoe et al, 2021).

