Data Gathering Procedure

	In gathering the data for this study, the researchers will follow the procedures below.

	Seeking Permission to Conduct the Study. To begin the study, the researchers will secure the necessary approvals from barangay officials and relevant authorities to ensure the smooth conduct of the research. They will also coordinate with disaster management personnel to guarantee that the system evaluation will not disrupt actual disaster operations.

	Seeking the Consent of the Respondents. The researchers will obtain informed consent from all participants before involving them in the study. Respondents will be informed about the purpose of the research, their role in the evaluation, and their right to withdraw participation at any time without consequence.

	Orienting the Respondents on the Nature and Purpose of the Study. Before answering the questionnaire, participants will be given a clear explanation of the study, including how the B-READY system works and why their feedback is important. They will be assured that all responses will remain confidential and used only for research purposes.

	Designing and Developing the Web-based System. The researchers will design and develop the B-READY system, which will allow real-time reporting and response during disasters at the barangay level. The system’s core features such as disaster incident reporting, response coordination, and data logging will be carefully tested before presenting it to the respondents for evaluation.

	Administering and Retrieving the Survey Questionnaire. The validated questionnaires will be distributed to selected barangay officials, staff, and community members. The researchers will guide respondents through the process, address their questions, and ensure honest and unbiased responses. Completed questionnaires will then be retrieved for analysis.

	Checking, Collating, and Processing the Data. The researchers will check the collected questionnaires for completeness and accuracy. Data will be carefully organized, tabulated, and statistically processed to generate meaningful results.

	Drawing Conclusions and Recommendations. Based on the analyzed data, the researchers will draw conclusions about the effectiveness of the B-READY system. They will also provide recommendations for further improvement and future development to strengthen barangay disaster preparedness and response.	

	System Testing and Evaluation. To ensure the reliability and practicality of the system, the researchers will conduct the following assessments:

	Accuracy Testing. The accuracy of the B-READY system will be measured by comparing reported incidents in the system with actual barangay records and official reports.

Efficiency Testing. The system’s efficiency will be tested by recording the time it takes to report an incident and generate alerts for barangay responders. Various conditions, such as internet speed and device type, will be considered.

Usability Testing. Respondents will evaluate the system’s ease of use, clarity of interface, and accessibility. Feedback will be gathered from both barangay staff and community residents.

Evaluation. The study and the system will be reviewed by a research evaluation committee to ensure that the objectives are met. Feedback from the committee will be considered for improving the system and ensuring its readiness for actual deployment.

Deployment. The researchers will plan a controlled deployment of the system in participating barangays. Proper documentation and orientation will be provided to ensure smooth integration into existing disaster management practices.

Data Analysis

	The goal will be to evaluate whether B-READY can accurately record and show real-time disaster reports from residents, make it easier to share information, and meet usability standards for both citizens and barangay officials. The analysis process will address each research question and test the hypotheses regarding the system’s performance.

Examine Data

	Accuracy Data. The accuracy of B-READY will be evaluated by comparing user-submitted disaster reports with verified records from barangay authorities. Metrics such as the percentage of correct reports, false reports, and duplicate entries will be used to assess the reliability of the system.

Efficiency Data. The system's speed at processing submitted reports, updating the database, and displaying alerts on the platform will be assessed by looking at the timestamps of each reporting step. The responsiveness of the reporting pipeline will be ascertained with the aid of this analysis.

Usability Data. Qualitative and quantitative feedback from barangay officials and residents will be gathered through surveys and interviews. The evaluation will focus on ease of use, clarity of instructions, works on different devices, and overall user satisfaction. This will provide insights into the system’s usability and areas for improvement.

Environmental/Contextual Data. External factors such as internet connectivity, device type (mobile or desktop), and disaster severity will be examined to assess their influence on reporting accuracy and timeliness. This analysis will determine the system’s effectiveness under varying real-world conditions.

Organize the Data

Categorization of Responses: The researchers will organize the collected data by grouping respondents according to their roles (e.g., barangay officials, disaster response personnel, and community residents), age groups, and level of experience in disaster management. This categorization will make it possible to analyze whether user background influences perceptions of the system’s effectiveness and usability.

Performance Tables. The researchers will summarize the results using tables that will present the mean scores and descriptive interpretations of the eight quality indicators from the ISO 25010 model: functional suitability, reliability, portability, usability, performance efficiency, security, compatibility, and maintainability. These tables will provide a structured overview of the B-READY system’s performance, showing both its strengths and the areas that will require improvement.

Statistical Data Grouping. For more detailed analysis, the researchers will group the responses according to feedback categories such as accuracy of reporting, timeliness of response, and ease of system use. Ratings will be further segmented based on user demographics and device type (e.g., mobile phone vs. desktop). This organization will allow for statistical comparisons and will help identify factors that affect the system’s performance in real-world scenarios.

Interpret the Data to Answer Research Questions

	The researchers will focus on evaluating the effectiveness of the B-READY web-based system in supporting barangay disaster reporting and response. Data will be gathered through survey questionnaires distributed to barangay officials, disaster response personnel, and community residents. Their responses will be analyzed based on the software quality ISO 25010 model, which includes the criteria of functional suitability, reliability, portability, usability, performance efficiency, security, compatibility, and maintainability.

	To assess accuracy, the researchers will compare the disaster reports logged in the B-READY system with the official barangay incident records. The consistency between the two datasets will provide evidence of the system’s reliability in capturing and transmitting disaster-related information in real-time.	The study will also examine efficiency, particularly the speed with which disaster incidents are reported and disseminated through the system. By recording the time from report submission to the generation of alerts, the researchers will be able to determine whether B-READY can facilitate timely responses under different conditions, such as varying internet speeds and device usage.

	To evaluate usability, the researchers will gather feedback from participants regarding their experience using the system. Questions will focus on ease of navigation, clarity of instructions, and accessibility for users with limited technical skills. This feedback will allow the researchers to assess how well the system can be adopted in real-world barangay settings.

	Mean. The mean will be used as a measure of central tendency to summarize the respondents' ratings for each of the eight quality indicators: Functional Suitability, Reliability, Portability, Usability, Performance Efficiency, Security, Compatibility, and Maintainability. This statistical metric will provide an overview of the average response for each indicator, allowing the researchers to determine whether the system is perceived as effective across all criteria. By calculating the mean score for each aspect covered in the ISO/IEC 25010 questionnaire adapted from the study ICTeachMUPO: An Evaluation of Information E-Learning Module System for Faculty and Students by Urera and Balahadia (2019), the researchers will be able to identify which areas of the B-READY system are strongest and which require further improvement. The mean will serve as a foundational tool for quantifying the overall user satisfaction and the system's perceived effectiveness from the perspective of barangay personnel and community members.

Standard Deviation. The standard deviation will serve as a measure of variability or dispersion in the respondents' ratings for each software quality indicator. A low standard deviation will indicate that the responses are consistent, suggesting a general agreement among the respondents about the system's performance for that specific characteristic. Conversely, a high standard deviation will point to greater variability in opinions, highlighting areas where user experiences diverge significantly (Turner, 2020). This information will be crucial for understanding the consensus among different stakeholder groups (e.g., ICT teachers, CDRRM officers, community members) and for identifying any specific features or issues that lead to disagreement. Insights gained from the standard deviation will guide targeted refinements to the B-READY system to ensure it meets the diverse needs and expectations of all end-users.

Drawing Conclusions	

	Drawing from the data interpretations, the researchers will assess the efficiency of the B-READY system in accomplishing its intended goals. Additionally, they will evaluate the need for any adjustments to enhance performance in suburban areas, including accuracy under varying emergency response and enhancement of user interface. The final report will determine whether the data supports the system's viability as a practical tool for reporting and coordinating real-time disaster responses.

Safety Precautions

Equipment safety. The researchers will make certain that every piece of equipment used is in top operating condition throughout the project and will receive routine inspections for maintenance. The need of adhering to validated reporting procedures will be emphasized in this course in order to avoid false alarms and guarantee the effective use of response resources. To guarantee accountability and offer a transparent audit trail for post-event assessment, every system access, report submissions, and alert escalations will be recorded. All barangay officials will receive the appropriate training, and there will be a transparent audit trail for the post-event review (Akinola et al., 2024).

Prototyping Safety. When creating prototypes or models for the B-READY: Real-time barangay Disaster Reporting and Response system, the researchers will be highly attentive to potential hazards associated with the tools, technologies, and materials used in the development process. Throughout this process, the research team will be highly attentive to potential hazards associated with the tools and materials used. Adopting a safety-first methodology, the team will carefully assess risks at each step of the prototyping workflow. The researchers will also conduct thorough training sessions to educate team members on the proper use of tools, devices, and laboratory resources, emphasizing safety protocols and best practices (Laura et al., 2025).

Data Security. The researchers will emphasize safeguarding confidential information and study results by following recognized data protection guidelines all through the project. The team will implement routine data backups and access control protocols to ensure the integrity and confidentiality of disaster reports. They will stay attentive to intellectual property rights related to the research, guaranteeing that all proprietary technology or discoveries are accurately recorded and safeguarded. These initiatives will guarantee that this system will function dependably even in crucial disaster scenarios. Through implementing these thorough actions, the researchers will develop a reliable atmosphere that will nurture confidence among participants and interested parties while preserving the authenticity of the research data(Condon et al., 2022).

Ethical Considerations

	In this study, ethical considerations will play an important role in guiding the researchers in their interactions with participants and in maintaining the integrity of the research. The researchers will ensure that only necessary information is collected and that sensitive data is kept secure to protect the privacy of barangay residents and officials. This study will also adhere to ethical principles to safeguard the rights and well-being of all participants, particularly the community members and responders involved in testing the B-READY system.

	Informed Consent. All respondents will be given clear information about the study’s objectives, procedures, and possible outcomes. This will ensure that they can make an informed decision about their participation. They will also be reminded that participation is voluntary and that they have the right to withdraw at any time without consequences (Millum & Bromwich, 2021).

Privacy and Confidentiality. Protecting the privacy of participants will be prioritized throughout the research. In compliance with Republic Act 10173, the Data Privacy Act of 2012, only essential information will be collected. Data will be anonymized, stored securely, and restricted to authorized personnel to prevent any breaches of confidentiality (Adarmouch et al., 2020).

Data Handling. Information gathered during the study will be handled responsibly. Access will be limited to the research team, and secure methods such as password-protected files and encrypted databases will be applied. Participants will also be informed that their data will be used only for academic and research purposes (Buhomoli & Muneja, 2022).

Beneficence. The research will be designed to minimize risks while maximizing benefits for the participants. Strategies will be applied to safeguard their well-being, and their involvement will contribute to creating outcomes that could improve community preparedness and resilience (Ajuwon, 2020). 

Respect for Persons. The researchers will respect the dignity and autonomy of all participants. Respondents will not be forced to take part, and decisions regarding their participation will be fully honored. The study will ensure that participants are engaged in a considerate and fair manner (Millum & Bromwich, 2020).

Fair Treatment. Recruitment and participation will be carried out fairly and without discrimination. All individuals will be given equal opportunity to join, ensuring that no group bears an unfair burden or is excluded from the benefits of participation (Government of Canada, 2023).

Transparency. The study will maintain openness with all participants by clearly communicating its goals, processes, and expected benefits. Findings will be reported in understandable terms to promote accountability and ensure that participants and stakeholders are well-informed (Malički et al., 2023).



	





